From 0bb686a9ae61084ce7160aa35913f8e993f55b2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:22:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Blu-ray: the pictures of a play item before its IN time are
 decoded and not presented

libbluray wipes the primary video of a play item up to its first picture presented at or after the IN
time. Where IN is not the time of a random-access picture, the pictures after IN are predicted from
pictures before it, which it wiped: the decoder, which waits for a random-access picture, presented the
play item only from its next one, up to a second later, and the Dolby Vision enhancement layer started
there too (its random-access picture came, the base picture that it pairs with did not). That is the
start of a play item whose IN the authoring did not put on a random-access picture, whether the
playlist reaches it or a menu jumps to it.

With libbluray-9091 (BLURAY_PLAYER_SETTING_VIDEO_PREROLL, asked for in SetupPlayerSettings(), in both
modes) libbluray gives the primary video of a play item that is not joined seamlessly from the
random-access picture at or before IN. The input says, for every byte it read, the IN time of the play
item it was read for (BlurayPreroll.h: CPresentationStarts, kept where the timeline of the joins is kept:
NavPlayItem(), NavRestart(), BeginClipSpan()). The demuxer marks a packet of the primary video presented
before the IN time of its play item, on the clock of its clip (DemuxPacket::decodeOnly,
MarkBlurayPreroll()); the decoder gives the mark to the picture of the packet through the opaque that
FFmpeg copies from packet to frame (beside the count of doviSourceEpoch), and GetPicture() leaves that
picture out after the pairing of the layers, as it leaves out a picture given again after a drain. So the
play item is presented from IN with every picture, and with its enhancement layer from the first one.
Nothing else is marked: the pictures of a seamless join, of audio, subtitles and menus, and every picture
when libbluray did not take the setting. kodi.log says the first marks of a demuxer and of a decoder, and
every hundredth. (A playlist played directly still loses the first bytes of a play item that does not
join seamlessly to the demuxer that BD_EVENT_DISCONTINUITY resets, as before: there this helps the first
play item and the ones a seek lands in.)

Run on the host (the join test, with libbluray-9091 and a disc
whose fourth playlist begins its second play item at the fourth picture of its clip, behind an EP map):
the play item's video begins at the random-access picture before IN, and the three video packets
presented before IN are marked, and only they; nothing of the other playlists is marked, also of a play
item kept at a seamless join whose clock is far below the IN time of the one before (40 checks). Three
mutations of the input and four of BlurayPreroll.h fail. The decoder's part is compiled with the commands
of the image's build, not run: no Blu-ray here.

The idea of decoding the pictures before IN and presenting none of them is CroqueMr's
(libreelec-x86-DV, for the clips of Dolby Vision); written anew for every primary video, in both modes.
